    <string name="home_today">Today</string>
    <string name="home_next_marker">Next</string>
    <!--
      The prayer whose time is in now — the one you can still pray. One word, because it
      sits in a pill beside a prayer name and a time on the narrowest phone we support, and
      because "Now" survives translation into a short word almost everywhere.

      Deliberately not "You can pray this now" or anything of that shape. That is a ruling,
      and this app is not a religious authority; it reports which prayer has most recently
      come in and leaves the ruling to the reader and their imam.
    -->
    <string name="home_now_marker">Now</string>
    <!-- Screen-reader label for the row carrying that pill. -->
    <string name="home_now_marker_a11y">Current prayer</string>
    <string name="notice_dismiss">Dismiss</string>
    <!-- Shown only above ~45° of latitude, and only while the method is still AUTO. The
         measured basis: the default (Muslim World League) puts Isha 78 minutes later than
         all three of the tester's Slough mosques, which follow Moonsighting Committee, but
         MWL is within about five minutes across South Asia, South-East Asia, the Middle
         East and Africa. So this is not "the default is wrong" — it is "the default has a
         latitude band where it stops matching local practice", and only that band is asked.

         It names no method and changes no time. Picking one for the user would be the app
         adopting a fiqh-adjacent position on their behalf, which is refused (HANDOVER §11,
         the rejected auto-switch option). Asking is not the same as deciding. -->
    <string name="method_notice_title">Does this match your mosque?</string>
    <string name="method_notice_body">This far from the equator, mosques differ most over when Fajr and Isha begin, sometimes by an hour or more. If these times do not match yours, tap to pick the method your mosque follows.</string>
    <string name="polar_notice_title">Approximate times</string>
    <!--
      Translators: %1$d is a latitude. It is 45 or 60 depending on the calculation method the
      user has chosen, so it must stay a placeholder and must not be written out as a word.
      Both figures come from a published ruling — see PrayerEngine.polarReferenceLatitude.
      The degree sign follows the number directly, as on the Qibla screen; keep them together.
      The last sentence is not padding: above the polar circles there is genuine scholarly
      disagreement, and sending the user to their own mosque is the honest answer.
    -->
    <string name="polar_notice_body">This far from the equator the sun does not always rise or set, so there is no true Fajr or Isha to calculate. These times are worked out from latitude %1$d° instead. Mosques here handle it in different ways, so ask yours what it follows.</string>
    <string name="home_no_location">Set your location to see prayer times.</string>
    <string name="home_countdown_a11y">%1$s in %2$s</string>
