    <string name="settings_alarm_respect_silent">Stay quiet when your phone is silent</string>
    <string name="settings_alarm_respect_silent_desc">On: you still get the notification on time, without the sound. Off: the alarm sounds even when the phone is silenced, the way a wake-up alarm does.</string>
    <string name="settings_dnd_title">Allow sound during Do Not Disturb</string>
    <string name="settings_dnd_desc">Without this, your alarm stays silent when the phone is on Do Not Disturb. Tap to allow.</string>

    <string name="settings_ongoing_badge">Keep the next prayer in the shade</string>
    <string name="settings_ongoing_badge_desc">A silent notification showing what is next and how long you have</string>
    <string name="settings_exact_alarms_title">Allow exact alarms</string>
    <!-- These two said only "and deliver them late", with no number, because Android
         documents no upper bound and "a few minutes" was a promise we could not keep.
         That was right while we were reasoning; it is wrong now that we have measured.
         Denying the permission was reproduced on two makes: a one-hour window on both a
         Galaxy S23 Ultra and a Redmi Note 13 Pro in daytime use, and on the Redmi
         overnight the alarm was parked roughly three days out by HyperOS and never fired
         at all. "Late" undersold that badly enough to talk a user out of a permission
         that silently costs them Fajr, so both strings now name the measured floor and
         the overnight case. Fajr is named because it is the one that matters and the one
         that fails. Evidence in HANDOVER section 10. -->
    <string name="settings_exact_alarms_desc">Without this your phone can hold prayer alerts back by an hour or more, and on some phones an overnight alert like Fajr may not arrive at all. Tap to allow.</string>
    <string name="confirm_exact_alarm_title">One last thing</string>
    <string name="confirm_exact_alarm_body">Android does not let apps set alerts for an exact minute unless you say so. Without it your phone can hold prayer alerts back by an hour or more, and on some phones an overnight alert like Fajr may not arrive at all. You can also do this later from Settings.</string>
